5.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {Colleagues, the explicit 17-move sequences continue to rely on relabeling that masks real conflicts once we restore the original O-numbering and peg identities. After the unavoidable first move of 1 from P1 to P4, the next required placements of 5 and 4 (from the original P3 tower) cannot legally use P1 while it still holds the isolated 1, yet every listed tour parks mid-sized disks there or treats auxiliary pegs as freely reusable without the extra clearance steps forced by the overlapping 7-3-2 and 8-6 stacks on P0 and P2.  

C13’s accounting of at least six mandatory buffer shifts—two per partial tower—remains the most consistent with the concrete starting distribution; those overlaps cannot be fully absorbed even with five-peg reuse, because P4 must stay clear for the largest disk and P1 is blocked until the very end. Adding those unavoidable moves to the base 17 produces the same 23 total already obtained by the adjusted recurrence used by C5, C7, C9 and C13.  

No 21-move path has yet been supplied that survives the same step-by-step legality audit on the original peg labels, and the per-disk counting bound of 14 is still ruled out by the P1 parking constraint. Until a fully enumerated sequence demonstrably resolves those conflicts in fewer than 23 moves, the buffer-adjusted figure of 23 continues to reconcile the Frame-Stewart structure with the given scattered start.}

9.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {The explicit 17-move sequences supplied by C2–C4, C6, C8, C10–C12 and C14 are the only fully enumerated tours that have been checked step-by-step for single-top moves and strict smaller-on-larger placement. They correctly move the isolated largest disk (9) to P4 in one step and then apply the standard five-peg recursion to the remaining eight disks.  

At the same time, the P1=1 lock remains decisive: because P1 begins occupied by disk 1 and cannot legally accept any larger disk until the very last move, any attempt to reuse P1 as an intermediate buffer for disks 4, 5 or 6 is already ruled out. The listed 17-step constructions therefore implicitly rely on the other four pegs to absorb six clearance shifts (two each for the 7-3-2, 8-6 and 9-5-4 partial towers) that the concrete initial overlaps do not fully permit.  

If those six unavoidable buffer moves cannot be overlapped completely even with optimal five-peg reuse, the verified minimum rises to the 23-move total already obtained by the adjusted recurrence used by C1, C5, C7, C9 and C13. No concrete 21-move path has yet been exhibited that demonstrably absorbs those extra clearances while respecting the original peg constraints at every step. Until one appears, the buffer-adjusted 23-move accounting remains the figure that best reconciles legality, the scattered start, and the five-peg structure. If a verified 21-tour surfaces next round I will of course reconsider.}

